<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walther von der Vogelweide</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1170 – c. 1230</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walther von der Vogelweide was the most celebrated German lyric poet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. He wrote love songs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesang">Minnesang</a> tradition as well as political poems commenting on the struggles between emperors and popes, and he served patrons including the Babenberg dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, Landgrave Hermann I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, who granted him a fief near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide.jpg" alt="Walther von der Vogelweide" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister des Codex Manesse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Manesse_Walther_von_der_Vogelweide.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
